\section{Diagnostic Framework}\label{sec:method}

Our analysis is observational: we characterize statistical associations between agreement signals and output quality without claiming causal mechanisms.

\subsection{Problem Formulation}\label{sec:formulation}

Given model $\mathcal{M}$ and input $x$, we sample $N$ outputs $\{y_1, \ldots, y_N\}$ at temperature $T > 0$ via constrained decoding \cite{willard2023guided}, where each $y_i$ is an entity set.
A \textbf{per-sample signal} $c_{\text{ps}}: (x, y_i) \to \mathbb{R}$ scores each output independently; an \textbf{instance-level signal} $c_{\text{il}}: (x, \{y_i\}_{i=1}^N) \to \mathbb{R}$ produces a single score per input (notation in Appendix~\ref{app:notation}).
This distinction is consequential: 4 of 5 signals are instance-level aggregates assigning identical scores to all $N$ candidates.
Only LP carries per-sample discriminative power: ensemble optimization assigns LP mean weight $0.78$, with structural signals receiving zero weight (Appendix~\ref{app:supervised_combination}).
When LP fails---as in few-shot regimes where LP $\rho{\to}0$ (\S\ref{sec:scaling})---no alternative per-sample signal exists.

We define the \textbf{capability ceiling} as the practical performance upper bound achievable through self-consistency at realistic sample budgets ($N{\leq}16$), beyond which diminishing returns render further scaling impractical.

\subsection{Confidence Signals}\label{sec:signals}

Entity matching uses exact span and type match.
We distinguish two signal families: four \textit{agreement signals} computed across samples---\textit{structural} (Soft Jaccard [SJ], entity voting confidence [VC]) and \textit{surface} (exact match [EM], Fleiss' $\kappa$ [FK])---and log-probability [LP] as the sole \textit{model-internal} signal, derived from generation probabilities and providing per-sample discrimination.

\paragraph{Log-probability (per-sample).}
$c_{\text{lp}}(y_i) = |y_i|^{-1} \sum_{j} \log p_{\mathcal{M}}(t_j \mid t_{<j}, x)$.
Mean LP is preferred over cumulative sequence-level LP as length normalization eliminates length bias ($+4.6$ F1 points; Appendix~\ref{app:token_signal}).

\paragraph{Soft Jaccard (structural, instance-level).}
For samples $y_i, y_j$, we group by type, compute max-weight bipartite matching (Hungarian algorithm, token-level IoU):
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sj}
\text{SJ}(y_i, y_j) = \frac{\sum_{m \in M} \text{sim}(a_m, b_m)}{\sum_{g} \max(|g_i|, |g_j|)}
\end{equation}
Instance-level QE averages over all pairs: $c_{\text{sj}}(x) = \binom{N}{2}^{-1} \sum_{i<j} \text{SJ}(y_i, y_j)$.
For selection, consensus-proximity converts to per-sample rankings via MBR (\S\ref{sec:mbr_theory}).

\paragraph{Other signals.}
EM: proportion of samples producing the most common output ($c_{\text{em}}(x) = N^{-1} \max_{y} |\{i : y_i = y\}|$).
FK: inter-rater agreement \cite{fleiss1971agreement} adapted to entity sets, treating unique (text, type) tuples as items with binary present/absent labels (Appendix~\ref{app:fk_independence}).
VC: mean entity frequency $\text{freq}(e) = |\{i : e \in y_i\}| / N$ across $E_\cup$.\footnote{When $E_\cup = \emptyset$, SJ${=}1$ (perfect agreement on empty output); $\text{VC}{=}0$.}

\subsection{Evaluation}\label{sec:evaluation}

\paragraph{Quality estimation.}
Spearman $\rho$ between signal and instance-level F1 (exact span-and-type match), and AUROC classifying above/below median F1.
Both exclude instances with no gold annotations: $n{=}529$ on SciERC, $n{=}2{,}756$ on CoNLL (Appendix~\ref{app:additional}).

\paragraph{Sample selection.}
Best-of-$N$ F1: LP ranks candidates directly; instance-level signals rank via consensus-proximity.
Baselines: \textit{oracle} (highest-F1 sample) and \textit{greedy} ($T{=}0$).
\textbf{Instance degeneracy}: fraction of instances where all $N$ samples yield identical F1;\footnote{``Identical F1'' means constant F1 across all $N$ samples for a given instance, not necessarily F1${=}1.0$.} for these, no signal can improve selection.

\section{Experiments}\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Datasets.}
We evaluate on four English NER benchmarks spanning a difficulty gradient.
\textbf{SciERC} \cite{luan2018scierc} (551 test instances, 6 entity types) is the primary medium-difficulty test bed (${\approx}$65\% F1).
\textbf{Few-NERD} \cite{ding2021fewnerd} (INTRA coarse; 37{,}648 instances, 8 entity types, ${\approx}$75\% F1) provides a low-degeneracy contrast where LP selection succeeds (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}).
\textbf{CoNLL-2003} \cite{tjongkimsang2003conll} (3{,}453 test sentences, 4 entity types, ${\approx}$91\% F1) serves as a ceiling-effect control.
\textbf{WNUT-17} \cite{derczynski2017wnut} (1{,}287 social media instances, 51\% zero F1) probes the signal-collapse boundary.

\paragraph{Models and sampling.}
\textbf{Qwen3-8B} and \textbf{LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct} are LoRA fine-tuned using LLaMA Factory \cite{zheng2024llamafactory} (Appendix~\ref{app:training}; Few-NERD uses 3 epochs vs.\ 5 for others; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).
$N{=}8$ samples at $T{=}1.0$ (primary) with vLLM constrained decoding \cite{willard2023guided}; $N{=}16$ for scaling (Appendix~\ref{app:n_scaling}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Quality Estimation Results}\label{sec:qe_results}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{NER quality estimation ($\rho$ and AUROC; $N{=}8$, primary seed${=}42$; 3-seed $\sigma{\leq}0.026$ in Table~\ref{tab:multiseed}). AUROC binarizes at per-dataset median F1. Best in \textbf{bold}. RE in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}; $N{=}16$ in Table~\ref{tab:robustness}.}
\label{tab:quality_estimation}
\begin{tabular}{ll cc cc cc}
\toprule
& & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{SciERC}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{CoNLL (Qwen)}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{CoNLL (LLaMA)}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){3-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-6} \cmidrule(lr){7-8}
Signal & Family & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC \\
\midrule
VC & Structural & \textbf{.379} & .684 & .424 & .789 & .426 & .709 \\
SJ  & Structural & .360 & .692 & .436 & .801 & \textbf{.431} & .704 \\
FK & Surface & .267 & .629 & .400 & .774 & .428 & .703 \\
EM  & Surface    & .295 & \textbf{.749} & \textbf{.451} & \textbf{.816} & \textbf{.431} & .705 \\
\midrule
LP     & Model      & .205 & .578 & .316 & .753 & .307 & \textbf{.761} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation} presents $\rho$ and AUROC for five signals across tasks and models.

\paragraph{Signal rankings are difficulty-dependent.}
On SciERC, structural signals lead (VC $\rho{=}0.379$, SJ $0.360$) over LP ($0.205$); bootstrap CIs confirm the gap ($\Delta\rho{=}{+}0.155$; Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_signal}).
On CoNLL (F1${\approx}$0.91), EM leads both $\rho$ and AUROC ($0.816$).
At the signal-collapse boundary (WNUT-17, F1${\approx}$42\%), full-set correlations are dominated by the zero/non-zero boundary (VC $\rho{=}0.724$ at $N{=}16$, but conditional $\rho{=}0.038$; Table~\ref{tab:wnut}); LP shows the reverse (conditional $\rho{=}0.350$; Figure~\ref{fig:signal_heatmap}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Cross-Dataset Contrast: Few-NERD}\label{sec:fewnerd}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{LP selection across datasets (Qwen3-8B, $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, gold-filtered, 3-seed means). $p$: paired bootstrap.}
\label{tab:fewnerd}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrccccr@{}}
\toprule
Dataset & $n$ & Greedy & Degen.\% & LP $\rho$ & LP $\Delta$ & $p$ \\
\midrule
WNUT-17 & 1{,}287 & .452 & 67.9\% & $-$.446 & $-$0.45\,pp & .72 \\
CoNLL & 2{,}756 & .908 & 61.7\% & .302 & $-$0.85\,pp & 1.0 \\
SciERC & 529 & .667 & 12.2\% & .159 & $+$0.61\,pp & .193 \\
Few-NERD & 32{,}565 & .748 & 13.0\% & .220 & $\mathbf{+1.39}$\,pp & ${<}.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

Few-NERD (Table~\ref{tab:fewnerd}) confirms LP selection at low degeneracy ($+1.39$~pp, $p{<}0.001$; structural signals degrade: VC $-1.79$, SJ $-2.14$~pp), with LP 50$\times$ more seed-stable ($\sigma{=}0.04$ vs.\ SJ $2.02$~pp), generalizing to LLaMA (Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}). Training convergence modulates degeneracy (5-epoch: $31$\%, halving LP gains; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).

Signal ranking is task-specific (EM dominates SciERC, SJ leads Few-NERD); per-type analysis shows MV degradation concentrates in low-F1 types ($\rho{=}0.90$, $p{=}0.002$) and 7/8 types reverse MV direction between pretrained and finetuned decoding (Appendix~\ref{app:pertype}), ruling out type-specific factors.

\paragraph{Degeneracy alone does not predict LP direction.}
Degeneracy does not predict LP gain across 23 configurations ($\rho{=}0.052$, $p{=}0.81$; $n{=}23$, insufficient for reliable correlation---we treat this as supplementary evidence; Figure~\ref{fig:degeneracy_scatter}), though it co-varies with LP within fixed settings ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_degeneracy_lp_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Instance degeneracy vs.\ LP selection gain across 23 configurations ($N{=}8$; $\rho{=}0.052$, $p{=}0.81$). Within Few-NERD per-entity-type ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}), degeneracy predicts LP gain when alignment is held constant.}
\label{fig:degeneracy_scatter}
\end{figure}

Structural signals remain negative regardless of sample budget; while the selection gap narrows with increasing $N$ (MV $\Delta$ improves from $-2.71$\,pp at $N{=}8$ to $-1.65$\,pp at $N{=}16$; Appendix~\ref{app:n_scaling}), diminishing returns suggest closing the gap requires impractical sample sizes.

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Robustness}\label{sec:robustness_main}

Signal rankings are robust across temperatures, seeds ($W{=}1.0$), and $N$ (Table~\ref{tab:robustness}); NER calibration is reasonable (ECE 0.069--0.096; Appendix~\ref{app:calibration}).

\paragraph{Scale and controlled ablations.}
Qwen3-4B QE is comparable to 8B ($|\Delta\rho|{=}0.042$; Table~\ref{tab:scale_ablation_app}), yet selection remains below greedy.
LoRA rank${=}8$ lowers degeneracy to 7.2\% but LP stays below greedy, confirming both conditions are necessary; supervised classifiers and hidden-state probes also fail (Appendix~\ref{app:supervised_combination}, \ref{app:hidden_probe}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Prescriptive Results}\label{sec:prescriptive}

\paragraph{Entity-level construction.}
We aggregate entities across samples, retaining those exceeding $\theta{=}2/N$, with LP-weighted or uniform scoring ($\theta$ stable in $[0.15, 0.35]$; Appendix~\ref{app:theta_sweep}). In zero-shot, LP-weighting is necessary (SciERC $+1.5$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale}).

LP-weighted construction improves on Few-NERD ($+1.40{\pm}0.51$\,pp, $p{<}0.001$; Holm $p_{\text{adj}}{=}0.006$, $k{=}10$; Table~\ref{tab:entity_construction}); SciERC shows a smaller, non-significant gain ($+0.32$\,pp, 3-seed); CoNLL shows no gain ($-0.39$\,pp), consistent with high degeneracy.
Gains require both low degeneracy \textit{and} sufficient oracle headroom (Few-NERD: $D{=}13$\%, headroom${=}12.4$\,pp).\footnote{Holm--Bonferroni \cite{holm1979simple} within families ($k{=}10$, $21$, $9$); global $k{=}57$: $p_{\text{adj}}{=}0.055$ (Appendix~\ref{app:holm}).}

\paragraph{Complementary strategies.}
Construction and LP selection are complementary, associated with within-instance LP alignment $\rho_{\text{adj}}$ (Few-NERD $0.516$ favors both; SciERC $0.167$ favors construction only).
QE signals also support selective prediction: at 80\% coverage, filtering yields $+5$--$8$\,pp F1 (AUPRC 0.70--0.92; Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}).

\phantomsection\label{sec:selection_results}

\section{Threshold Regime Analysis}\label{sec:theory_scaling}

\subsection{Entity Construction as MBR Decoding}\label{sec:mbr_theory}

Entity-level construction selects $\hat{y}_{\textup{MBR}} = \{e \in E_\cup : \hat{p}(e) > \alpha/(\alpha{+}1)\}$ under balanced set-overlap utility; $\alpha{=}1$ recovers majority vote (Proposition~\ref{prop:construction_mbr}; Table~\ref{tab:entity_construction}; Appendix~\ref{app:mbr_proof}).
Signal-derived weights $w_i$ yield quality-weighted MBR: $\hat{p}_w(e) = \sum_{i} w_i \, \mathbf{1}[e {\in} y_i] / \sum_{i} w_i$; weighting improves when $w_i$ correlates with instance quality but degrades otherwise (LP on SciERC: $\rho{\approx}0.2$; \S\ref{sec:prescriptive}).
We select $\varepsilon{=}0.05$ at the elbow of the compression curve, where LP-best selection advantage transitions from $+1.5$\,pp (compressed) to $+4.6$\,pp (non-compressed; Appendix~\ref{app:fewnerd_epsilon}).

\subsection{Scaling and Inverse Scaling}\label{sec:scaling}

Oracle F1 follows $F_1(N) = a - b \cdot N^{-c}$ ($R^2 > 0.999$; Figure~\ref{fig:oracle_scaling}): Few-NERD ($c{\approx}0.61$) scales comparably to reasoning tasks \cite{brown2024monkeys}, SciERC converges more slowly ($c{\approx}0.35$), yet confidence-based aggregation recovers ${<}1$\,pp---a persistent gap.
The gap persists across scales and families: scaling from 8B to 14B FT increases oracle headroom ($+1.2$\,pp) with minimal greedy gain ($+0.9$\,pp), and GPT-5.5 (200 Few-NERD instances, zero-shot) exhibits the same pattern---50\% degeneracy, SJ $\rho{=}0.346$, MV $+1.4$\,pp yet 6.8\,pp oracle headroom unrealized.\footnote{10.1\% of GPT-5.5 samples produced unparseable output; these are excluded from all metrics. See Appendix~\ref{app:frontier_sc}.}
Degeneracy is model-family-dependent rather than scale-dependent (Qwen2.5: $31.7$--$46.2$\% vs.\ Qwen3: $13.0$\% vs.\ GPT-5.5: $50$\% on Few-NERD; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale}).

\paragraph{Inverse scaling under adaptive thresholds.}
Increasing $N$ \textit{decreases} construction F1 in 40 of 58 transitions ($p{<}0.05$; Appendix~\ref{app:n_scaling}).
Methods using $\theta{=}2/N$ are most affected ($-3.09$\,pp at $N{=}8$, $-6.90$\,pp at $N{=}16$ vs.\ greedy on Few-NERD); majority vote ($\theta{=}\tfrac{1}{2}$) reduces the delta to $-0.87$/$-0.60$\,pp---severity reduction, not immunity (Table~\ref{tab:theta_sweep}).
Few-shot demonstrations improve QE (CoNLL SJ $\rho$ $.30{\to}.44$) but not construction (all n.s.; Appendix~\ref{app:fs_error}).

\subsection{Degeneracy Decomposition}\label{sec:theory}

\noindent\textbf{Degeneracy Decomposition.}\label{thm:degeneracy_bound}
For any aggregation method $A$: $\mathbb{E}[\Delta F_1(A)] \leq (1 - D) \cdot G_{\textup{nd}}$,
where $D$ is the degeneracy rate and $G_{\textup{nd}}$ the oracle gap on non-degenerate instances.
For signal-based methods with alignment $\alpha \in [0,1]$, this tightens to $(1{-}D) \cdot G_{\textup{nd}} \cdot \alpha$ (proof in Appendix~\ref{app:bound_proof}).

\noindent The decomposition is diagnostic rather than predictive: the three factors ($D$, $G_{\textup{nd}}$, $\alpha$) localize where aggregation fails rather than forecasting performance.
Across 16 configurations, all gains respect the bound but it captures only 3--12\% of actual gains (Figure~\ref{fig:bound}): $\alpha$---not $D$---is the bottleneck ($\alpha{=}0.000$--$0.161$), and the bound cannot be tightened by signal combination (\S\ref{sec:robustness_main}) or verifier-based construction (\S\ref{sec:verifier}).
The bound assumes entity-wise independence (a simplification; actual entity correlations further attenuate $\alpha$, as the 3--12\% ratio indirectly reflects).

\begin{proposition}[Entity Selection Diagnostic]\label{prop:entity_selection_bound}
Let $\mathcal{M}^+ {=} \hat{y}_{\textup{MV}} \setminus \hat{y}_{\textup{gr}}$ be entities added by majority vote (precision $\pi_{\mathcal{M}^+}$) and $\delta$ the instance degeneracy rate.
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textup{(\alph*)},nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item On degenerate instances, $\hat{y}_{\textup{MV}} {=} \hat{y}_{\textup{gr}}$ and $\Delta F_1 {=} 0$.
\item $\mathbb{E}[\Delta F_1] = (1{-}\delta) \cdot \mathbb{E}[\Delta F_1 \mid \textup{non-deg.}]$.
\item $\Delta F_1 > 0$ only if $\pi_{\mathcal{M}^+} > \alpha_{\textup{crit}} \triangleq F_1^{\textup{gr}}/2$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}
\noindent\textit{Proof in Appendix~\ref{app:alpha_crit}.}
Table~\ref{tab:alpha_crit} validates: MV fails to improve F1 on any dataset ($\Delta$ from $-2.74$ to ${\approx}0$\,pp).
Defining $\alpha_{\textup{crit,eff}} {=} \alpha_{\textup{crit}} / (1{-}\delta)$, CoNLL ($\alpha_{\textup{crit,eff}}{=}1.148 {>} 1$) makes MV gain structurally impossible; the observed $\Delta{=}{-}0.04$\,pp confirms this.
Few-NERD and SciERC ($\alpha_{\textup{crit,eff}} {<} 1$) are negative ($-2.74$, $-0.74$\,pp): even where structural headroom exists, marginal-entity precision is insufficient.

\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Test-time compute scaling.}
Self-consistency \cite{wang2023selfconsistency} and best-of-$N$ \cite{snell2025scaling, brown2024monkeys} drive large gains on reasoning and code \cite{chen2021codex}.
Reward-free selectors \cite{taubenfeld2025cisc, kang2025selfcertainty}, BoN scaling laws \cite{gao2023scaling}, adaptive allocation \cite{aggarwal2023adaptive}, and trained verifiers \cite{cobbe2021verifiers, lightman2024verify} all target discrete-answer settings; structured prediction requires different aggregation because degeneracy operates at the entity-set level.

\paragraph{SC failure modes and fine-grained variants.}
\citet{chen2023twofailures} identify SC failures where consistent outputs are systematically wrong; \citet{huang2024large} show LLMs cannot self-correct reasoning without external feedback. Our correlation-selection gap is a distinct failure where signals \textit{correlate} with quality yet fail to \textit{select}.
\citet{chen2024usc} extend SC to free-form tasks; our diagnostic shows such extensions face limits under constrained decoding (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}). \citet{thirukovalluru2024atomic} decompose claims atomically; our entity-level decomposition shows sub-output agreement does not translate to selection gain.

\paragraph{Consistency for structured IE.}
\citet{yao2026consistency} demonstrate that consistency correlates with quality across NER/RE tasks ($\rho$ up to 0.47); we diagnose why this correlation fails to yield selection gain, identifying entity-level mechanisms (degeneracy, LP compression, near-zero $\alpha$) absent from their task-level analysis, and provide an operational workaround via entity construction ($+0.32$--$1.40$\,pp) at entity granularity.
\citet{xie2023zeroshot} propose entity voting for zero-shot NER, \citet{li2024simple} improve structured output formatting, and \citet{xie2024selfimproving} report positive SC results under zero-shot prompting where output diversity is high---contrasting with our fine-tuned setting where degeneracy inverts the diversity conditions.
Our approach builds on generative IE \cite{zhou2024uniner, sainz2024gollie} and constrained decoding \cite{willard2023guided, dong2024xgrammar}.

\paragraph{Uncertainty estimation and MBR.}
Our selection instantiates MBR decoding \cite{eikema2022sampling, denero2009fast}; our MV is a special case under set-overlap utility that fails to improve over greedy. Despite MBR's success in translation \cite{freitag2022high} and code generation \cite{jinnai2024mbr}, all variants fail here due to instance degeneracy (Appendix~\ref{app:mbr_connection}). \citet{eikema2025structured} find MBR utilities degrade on structured outputs; our analysis localizes failure via task-agnostic diagnostics and a four-stage cascade (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}).
Semantic entropy \cite{kuhn2023semantic, farquhar2024detecting} clusters generations by meaning, but equivalence classes are ill-defined for entity sets with partial overlap.
Calibration \cite{kuleshov2015calibrated, desai2020calibration}, confidence estimation \cite{geng2024confidence, xia2025survey}, and selective prediction \cite{geifman2017selective, varshney2022selective, fisch2021conformal} provide complementary model-internal alternatives; our work analyzes agreement patterns without requiring such access, with LP compression (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}) identifying a practical failure mode.

\paragraph{Capability ceiling vs.\ diversity collapse.}\label{sec:yun_contrast}
\citet{yun2025price} identify \textit{diversity collapse} under constrained decoding; our LP compression (median 0.041 nats) corroborates this but differs in scope (agreement-quality correlation vs.\ generation diversity), mechanism (model-internal), and diagnostic depth (four-stage cascade); Table~\ref{tab:differentiation} provides the full comparison.

\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\label{sec:discussion}

Degeneracy (12.2--67.9\%) and LP-quality alignment ($\rho$ 0.043--0.667) constrain aggregation; temperature disentangles these (\S\ref{sec:scaling}; Table~\ref{tab:t_degeneracy}).
Across four frontier models, degeneracy and the selection gap are negatively correlated (5.5--74\%; Table~\ref{tab:frontier_sc_cross}), consistent with the bound (\S\ref{sec:theory}).

\paragraph{Pretrained vs.\ finetuned.}
On CoNLL, the pretrained model's MV captures ${\sim}98$\% of oracle headroom (degeneracy $36$\%) versus ${<}2$\% finetuned ($57$--$60$\%); fine-tuning decouples agreement from quality through dataset-dependent pathways (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale}), with 7/8 entity types flipping MV direction after finetuning (Appendix~\ref{app:pertype}).

\paragraph{From diagnosis to practice.}\label{sec:selection_method}\label{sec:conclusion}
Entity-level construction bypasses both failure mechanisms when degeneracy ${<}22$\% ($p{<}0.01$; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).
We position these gains ($+0.3$--$1.4$\,pp) as diagnostic evidence: aggregation fails above $\theta_{\text{crit}}{=}0.70$, and a major contributing factor is near-zero $\alpha$ even when $\rho$ is substantial, compounded by LP compression \cite{kadavath2022language, xiong2024verbalized}.

\section{Where Single-Signal Selection Fails}\label{sec:selection_gap}

\paragraph{Definitional vs.\ empirical.}
We decompose the correlation-selection gap into two layers.
The first is structural: four of five signals are instance-level aggregates that assign identical scores to all $N$ candidates from a given input (\S\ref{sec:formulation}), so they estimate quality across inputs but cannot rank candidates within an instance---selection failure is a design property, not an empirical finding.
The second layer is empirical and constitutes the core diagnostic: LP---the only per-sample signal---suffers compression under constrained decoding, with 60\% of SciERC instances showing within-instance LP range below 0.041 nats (Appendix~\ref{app:logprob_ties}), effectively eliminating the variation needed for sample discrimination. Even where LP varies, selection gains depend on LP-quality alignment rather than degeneracy alone.
Cross-instance $\rho$ (Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation}) is attenuated by varying instance difficulty; within-instance $\rho$ directly measures whether LP discriminates candidates for a fixed input, making it the relevant predictor of selection success.

